{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791606374,"nonce":"WBVE4eUSZvxhv6PyfkgJeP0oPws=","title":"Start-up makes three Cranelift JIT modules where one serves","message":"A tiered runtime's start-up made a JIT module in TieredBackend::new, then rebuilt it in TieredRuntime::new and again in register_static_plugins, because a runtime symbol registered after the module exists was only resolvable by building a new module with every symbol given to it again. Each module reserves its own address space (an mmap) and re-adds every runtime symbol, and neither rebuild had anything defined in the module to keep.\n\nFix: the backend keeps every registered runtime symbol in a table the JIT module's symbol lookup consults first, so a symbol given late resolves in the module that exists; rebuild_with_accumulated_symbols returns at once when the module defines nothing yet (no functions, globals, compiled functions or exports).\n\nMeasured on the Mac against cc5bef68 (release, llvm-backend build, LLVM tier off):\n- fib start-up repeated 4000 times, sampled 5 s: rebuild_with_accumulated_symbols 155 samples -\u003e 0, jit_builder 130 -\u003e 71, mmap 200 -\u003e 69, of about 6300 on the start-up thread.\n- Retired instructions, start-up only (ZYLUA_PROFILE_STARTUP=10 / ZYPY_PROFILE_STARTUP=10, median of 3): Lua 17 benchmarks 28.163G -\u003e 28.112G (-0.18%; fib -0.6%, closure_call -0.6%); Python 8 kernels 40.723G -\u003e 40.694G (-0.07%). Most of what goes is kernel work (address-space reservation), which instructions barely show.\n- Wall, 100 start-ups per process, 10 interleaved rounds, medians: fib 0.244 -\u003e 0.240 s (-1.6%), closure_call 0.278 -\u003e 0.270 (-3.0%), nbody 3.620 -\u003e 3.604 (-0.4%), method_call 5.191 -\u003e 5.202 (+0.2%, noise).","files":null}]}